---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'howitworks' as const;
---
<Base lang={lang} page={page}
  title="How it works"
  description="The extension helps reviewers write Conventional Comments as they work. The optional server checks the pull request independently. See what each component does and why both exist.">
<section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g18 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow acc">how it works:</span>
          <h1 style="font-size:clamp(29px,3.8vw,41px);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.031em;line-height:1.05">The extension helps you write. The server checks afterwards.</h1>
          <p class="lede">The browser extension makes Conventional Comments easy to use while you're reviewing: a toolbar, keyboard shortcuts, and feedback as you type. The server is optional. If your organization deploys one, it independently re-evaluates the pull request and publishes a compliance check. They serve different purposes. Here's what each one actually does.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">in the editor:</span>
          <h2>What the extension adds to the page</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="feat">
          <div>
            <h3>A two-row toolbar</h3>
            <p>Labels are on the first row. Decorations are on the second. They stay separate regardless of the available width because choosing a label and qualifying it with a decoration are two different actions. Putting everything in one wrapping row would eventually place the two side by side as though they meant the same thing.</p>
            <p>Clicking a label <strong>inserts or replaces the prefix</strong> without touching the rest of what you've written. Click the same label again to remove it. Hover over any label to see its definition and an example.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Keyboard controls</h3>
            <p>If you prefer the keyboard, type <code>/</code> or <code>:</code> at the beginning of the comment box to open a filterable list. Use <code>↑</code> and <code>↓</code> to navigate, <code>Enter</code> or <code>Tab</code> to select, and <code>Esc</code> to close it.</p>
            <p>You can also expand abbreviations with <code>Tab</code>:</p>
            <pre class="code">?i  → issue:
?ib → issue (blocking):</pre>
            <p>Direct shortcuts such as <code>Alt+I</code> are available as well.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Live validation below the comment box</h3>
            <p>A status indicator tells you whether the comment is:</p>
            <ul class="bul">
              <li>compliant;</li>
              <li>compliant with warnings; or</li>
              <li>non-compliant.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>If several problems are present, the extension shows <strong>all of them at once</strong>. Showing only one would simply force you to fix issues one after another to discover what comes next.</p>
            <p>Validation is debounced by 150&nbsp;ms, so the interface remains stable while you type.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Badges on published comments</h3>
            <p>Once comments are published, their labels are rendered as badges without modifying the stored comment itself. A banner at the top of the pull request shows how many unresolved blocking threads remain and links directly to them.</p>
            <p>That count comes from the summary published by the server, so the banner and the compliance check report the same state.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">a deliberate choice:</span>
          <h2>There is no “publish anyway” button.</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15.5px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">In its strictest mode, the extension can prevent a malformed comment from being submitted. There is no local override button. That's deliberate, for two reasons.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="duo">
          <div>
            <span class="tag" style="color:var(--fg-muted)">first</span>
            <h3>A bypass button wouldn't provide real control</h3>
            <p>A browser extension can be disabled in a couple of clicks. Someone determined to bypass it already can. Adding a dedicated bypass button would not create any meaningful new capability — only an extra ceremony, a reason field, another log, and the appearance of control where none actually exists.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="tag" style="color:var(--accent)">more importantly</span>
            <h3>The justification belongs in the review</h3>
            <p>“We decided not to address this point, and here's why” is part of the review itself. It should appear as a labelled comment in the thread, visible to everyone and preserved with the pull request. It should not disappear into a separate technical audit log.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout acc">
          <span class="ct">so what is the extension?</span>
          <h4>A guardrail, not a wall.</h4>
          <p>The extension catches mistakes and lapses of attention — the common case. It is not intended to stop someone who deliberately chooses to bypass it. That's where the server matters: it runs independently of every reviewer's browser.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">when the extension is allowed to block:</span>
          <h2>Four conditions must be met</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">Strict mode alone is not enough. Unless all four conditions below are satisfied, the extension reports diagnostics but still allows the comment to be submitted.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li><span class="mk">1</span><b>Strict mode is actually in effect</b><span class="tx">The effective mode is resolved from the organization's configuration. It is never assumed locally.</span></li>
          <li><span class="mk">2</span><b>The pull request is within scope</b><span class="tx">If a repository starts enforcing the convention from a particular date, older pull requests do not suddenly become blocked retroactively.</span></li>
          <li><span class="mk">3</span><b>The extension and server agree on the configuration</b><span class="tx">Both publish a fingerprint of the configuration they are applying. If the fingerprints differ — for example while a stricter configuration is still propagating — the extension stops blocking submissions and reports the mismatch.</span></li>
          <li><span class="mk">4</span><b>The configuration was successfully read</b><span class="tx">If the configuration cannot be retrieved, the extension does not reject anything. A user should never be blocked in the name of a rule the extension itself could not read.</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small measure">The last three conditions all protect the same principle: <strong>the extension must never reject locally what the authoritative evaluation would allow.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">on the server:</span>
          <h2>What the compliance check actually evaluates</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">Two things. Nothing more.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="duo">
          <div>
            <span class="tag" style="color:var(--fg-muted)">first</span>
            <h3>Are the comments correctly formed?</h3>
            <p>A comment is compliant when it produces no error-level diagnostic. Warnings are reported and counted, but do not fail the check by themselves. Most teams keep formatting errors non-blocking.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="tag" style="color:var(--accent)">second</span>
            <h3>Have all blocking points been resolved?</h3>
            <p>A thread becomes blocking when its comment resolves to a blocking verdict. Once blocking, it remains blocking until either the thread is resolved, or an authorized person records a <code>decision</code> explaining why the point will not be addressed.</p>
            <p class="guar">This is the criterion that can prevent the merge.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="check">
          <div class="cr">
            <span class="ic" style="background:var(--pass)"></span>
            <span class="tx"><b>conventional-comments</b> — all blocking comments resolved<span>Required · merging is allowed</span></span>
          </div>
          <div class="mach">cc/1 core=1.0.0 mode=enforce blocking=0 fmt=ok fp=7f3c…a91</div>
        </div>
        <p class="small measure">The final line is machine-readable. It records which core version performed the evaluation, which mode was active, how many unresolved blocking points remain, and the fingerprint of the configuration that was applied. That fingerprint is what allows the extension to detect when it and the server are no longer evaluating against the same configuration.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">why the two sides stay consistent:</span>
          <h2>They run the same validation code.</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15.5px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">This isn't two separate implementations of the same rules that have to be kept in sync. The extension and server consume <strong>the same validation package</strong>, and no validation rule exists outside it. Given the same configuration, a comment accepted by the extension is accepted by the server, and vice versa.</p>
          <p style="font-size:15.5px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">The validation library itself knows nothing about browsers or networks. Everything it needs is passed in as data, and everything it wants the caller to do comes back as a list of actions. That separation is also why the same validation engine can run directly in the browser — and why the <em>playground</em> is possible.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta-band">
      <div class="cta-in">
        <div class="stack g8">
          <h2>The extension is all you need to get started.</h2>
          <p class="small" style="color:var(--fg-2)">Deploy the server when your team decides the convention should be checked independently of each reviewer's browser.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-btns">
          <a class="btn btn-p" href={href('install', 'en')}>Install the extension</a>
          <a class="btn btn-s" href={href('server', 'en')}>Deploy the server</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
</Base>
